course: dictionary
lesson: 10584
title: Underemployed: work, but not enough
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Underemployed is a long word, but you can break it into parts. Under- often means less than, as in underfed. Employed means having paid work. So underemployed means having some work, but less than you want or less than your skills allow. «Many young people are underemployed.»
explain: The sentence tells you these young people have work, but not enough of it, or not the kind of work that uses what they can do. Check your meaning by swapping it in: «Many young people have some work, but not enough.» If that still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
explain: Do not mix it up with unemployed, which means having no job at all. A person who works three hours a week may be underemployed. A person with no work at all is unemployed.
fact: Underemployed is an adjective, so it describes a person or a group of people.
fact: «Underemployed» means having fewer hours than you want, or doing work that does not use your skills or training.
fact: The prefix under- often means less than, as in underfed or undercooked.
fact: Unemployed means having no job at all. Underemployed means having some work, but not enough or not the right kind.
fact: The word is often used in news and economics, and sometimes in maths problems about workers with fewer hours.
exercise: «Many young people are underemployed.» What does underemployed mean? | Too young to work | Having no job at all | Working very hard for long hours | *Having some work, but fewer hours or less use of your skills than you want
exercise: What does the start of the word, under-, suggest? | Very loud | *Less than enough | Very old | More than enough
exercise: Ella works 10 hours a week but wants 30 hours. Is she underemployed? | Yes, but only if she is still at school | *Yes, she has fewer hours than she wants | No, she works too much | No, because she has no job at all
test: A teacher with a degree now works as a cleaner because no teaching jobs are free. Which word fits best? | *Underemployed | Retired | Famished | Unemployed
test: Which word is closest in meaning to underemployed? | Overworked | Unpaid for every hour | *Not fully used | Fully used
exam: Which sentence uses underemployed correctly? | *Many graduates are underemployed, working in shops when they trained as engineers. | My dog is underemployed because he is very old. | The bus was underemployed because it was full of passengers. | The lake was underemployed, so the fish swam away.
